The ASCII concrete suite completes with `.K`, `NoExc`, and exit code 0; the Unicode-literal run remains a documented supplied-semantics limitation. I’m now checking real-program pinning by macro-expanding both `solution.mpy` and the claim’s `proof-program.mpy` under the fresh definition, then performing a body mutation inside the K term actually used by the claim.
exec
/bin/bash -lc "script -q -e -c 'kast solution.mpy --definition verification-kompiled-fresh --module VERIFICATION --sort Module --expand-macros --output kore > solution-expanded.kore; kast proof-program.mpy --definition verification-kompiled-fresh --module VERIFICATION --sort Module --expand-macros --output kore > proof-program-expanded.kore; cmp solution-expanded.kore proof-program-expanded.kore; wc -c solution-expanded.kore proof-program-expanded.kore; sha256sum solution-expanded.kore proof-program-expanded.kore' /audit-output/evidence/04-program-term-identity.log" in /tmp/audit-work/proof
